@*
    Backup page (M.1c.3). One purpose: let an operator pull a
    redacted, signed-audit-attached backup of the gateway as a single
    zip download.

    Restore is deliberately absent — lands in M.2a with the
    configuration draft/apply pipeline. A placeholder card tells the
    operator the workflow without claiming a capability the runtime
    doesn't have yet.

    The download is a plain anchor to /api/v1/backup with the
    `download` attribute. The browser handles streaming, filename
    (from Content-Disposition), and the "save as" dialog. No JS
    gymnastics, no Blob handling.
*@
@page "/backup"

<PageTitle>Backup · Connectivity Studio</PageTitle>

<div class="page-heading">
    <span class="page-heading__icon"><MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.SettingsBackupRestore" Size="Size.Medium" /></span>
    <div class="page-heading__text">
        <div class="page-heading__title">Backup</div>
        <div class="page-heading__desc">
            Download a complete backup of your gateway configuration, change history, and audit log.
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<MudGrid Spacing="3">
    @* ---- Download card (the marquee) ---- *@
    <MudItem xs="12" md="8">
        <MudPaper Elevation="1" Class="pa-6">
            <MudStack Row="true" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Spacing="2" Class="mb-4">
                <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Archive" Size="Size.Medium" Color="Color.Primary" />
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Download backup</MudText>
            </MudStack>

            @* Says what the file DOES for the operator, not how it is built. The
               zip's internal filenames, its checksum scheme and the JSONPath
               redaction map are support concerns; they moved to the expander
               below so they are still reachable without leading with them. *@
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mb-3">
                A single file containing everything needed to rebuild this gateway's
                configuration — every source, destination and route, plus the full
                history of changes and who made them.
            </MudText>

            @* Warning, not Info: this is a caveat that changes what the operator
               must do later, and it was previously a subordinate clause third in
               an info box. Restoring onto a rebuilt machine with no credentials
               and no trusted certificates is the difference between a backup that
               works and a bad afternoon — so it leads. *@
            <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Warning" Variant="Variant.Outlined" Class="my-4"
                      data-testid="backup-secrets-notice">
                <strong>Passwords and certificates are not included.</strong>
                <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mt-1">
                    They are removed before the file is created, so it can be stored
                    or sent to Elpis support without exposing credentials.
                </MudText>
                <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Class="mt-1">
                    When this configuration is restored on another machine you will
                    need to re-enter each destination's password and re-trust its
                    certificates before data flows again.
                </MudText>
            </MudAlert>

            @* Kept for whoever opens the zip — support, or an auditor checking
               what left the gateway. Closed by default; native <details> so it
               survives a re-render and needs no component state. *@
            <details class="mb-4" data-testid="backup-contents">
                <summary style="cursor:pointer;">What's inside the file</summary>
                <ul style="margin:0.5rem 0 0; padding-left:1.5rem;">
                    <li><strong>gateway.json</strong> — the current configuration, with secrets removed</li>
                    <li><strong>history/*.json</strong> — every configuration that was previously applied</li>
                    <li><strong>audit.log</strong> — the full record of who changed what, and when</li>
                    <li><strong>manifest.json</strong> — checksums, gateway identity, and a list of exactly which values were removed</li>
                </ul>
            </details>

            <MudStack Row="true" Spacing="2">
                <MudButton Href="/api/v1/backup"
                           Target="_blank"
                           Variant="Variant.Filled"
                           Color="Color.Primary"
                           Size="Size.Large"
                           StartIcon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Download"
                           Style="text-transform:none;">
                    Download backup
                </MudButton>
                @* The literal template placeholders ({gatewayId}, {ISO timestamp})
                   were printed as-is, so the caption showed a filename that never
                   appears on disk. Describe the shape instead — the point is only
                   to help the operator find it in their Downloads folder. *@
                <MudText Typo="Typo.caption" Color="Color.Secondary" Class="d-flex align-center">
                    Saved with this gateway's name and today's date in the filename.
                </MudText>
            </MudStack>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>

    @* ---- Restore placeholder ---- *@
    <MudItem xs="12" md="4">
        <MudPaper Elevation="1" Class="pa-6" Style="height:100%;">
            <MudStack Row="true" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Spacing="2" Class="mb-3">
                <MudIcon Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.RestorePage" Size="Size.Medium" Color="Color.Default" />
                <MudText Typo="Typo.h6">Restore</MudText>
            </MudStack>

            @* "Milestone M.2a" is an internal release identifier — it appeared
               here twice and meant nothing to the person holding the backup.
               What they need is the fact (restore is not self-service yet) and
               the way forward (support will do it), not a description of how the
               unbuilt feature will work. *@
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body2" Color="Color.Secondary">
                Restoring from a backup isn't available in the Studio yet. Send the
                backup file to Elpis support and they will restore it for you.
            </MudText>

            <MudTooltip Text="Not available yet — send the backup file to Elpis support to have it restored.">
                <span>
                    <MudButton Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                               Disabled="true"
                               StartIcon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Upload"
                               Class="mt-3"
                               Style="text-transform:none;">
                        Restore from backup
                    </MudButton>
                </span>
            </MudTooltip>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>
